// v3 Boundary Specification
//
// This document defines what each compiler layer OWNS, what facts are
// AUTHORITATIVE at each boundary, and what is FORBIDDEN from leaking
// across boundaries. It is a contract document, not a type catalog.
//
// Implementation follows from these contracts. Types serve the contracts,
// not the other way around.
//
// KEY INVARIANT: v3 keeps the DAG worldview and drops the single-schema
// pipeline. Each layer has its own graph schema. No stage reads fields
// that a prior stage "happens to have set."

module v3.spec

// =========================================================================
// 1. GOAL
// =========================================================================
//
// The compiler is specified as four IR layers:
//
//   1. Authored graph
//   2. Semantic graph
//   3. Analysis views
//   4. Render plan
//
// Every downstream decision reads from an explicit contract instead of
// reconstructing meaning from Node, names, or target-specific heuristics.
//
// Boundary sufficiency rule: if a downstream stage branches on a name,
// inspects raw type-node children for "Unit", or infers callability from
// fallback lookups, the boundary is insufficient. Fix the boundary.

// =========================================================================
// 2. WHY V2 IS NOT ENOUGH
// =========================================================================
//
// v2 proved the product thesis (structural glue generation works) but
// its Node still conflates authored identity, semantic facts, execution
// shape, and metadata. Node carries name, ident, span, children, params,
// inferred, uses, body, transport, properties, type_annotation,
// match_pattern, and expr_data all in one type. That pressure turns
// "DAG as IR" into "Node as god struct."
//
// v2 also loses information at the reconcile→emit boundary:
// - Rust emit classifies field access and optional receiver behavior
// - lookup_in_scope fabricates function-as-value references from returns
// - Dynamic compatibility masks missing normalization
// - empty_map() and fold accumulator types lack expected-type propagation
// - VariantPattern bindings in match arms are skipped by ownership
//
// These are not renderer bugs. They are proof that the semantic boundary
// is insufficient.

// =========================================================================
// 3. IR LAYER CONTRACTS
// =========================================================================

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3a. Authored graph
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Records exactly what the user wrote.
//
// OWNS:
//   - source spans
//   - authored names
//   - token/syntax sugar
//   - surface pattern structure
//   - parse-local grouping
//
// DOES NOT OWN:
//   - resolved references
//   - final types
//   - ownership
//   - complexity
//   - target rendering choices
//
// Authored names exist here as provenance only. They are NOT semantic
// authority. If scrambling names changes downstream behavior, the
// boundary is insufficient and names are being used as proxies.

type AuthoredNode {
  id: NodeId
  span: SourceSpan
  name_span: SourceSpan?

  // Structure
  children: List<AuthoredNode>
  connective: Connective            // Conj | Disj | Arrow | NoConnective
  params: List<AuthoredNode>
  body: AuthoredNode?
  return_cardinality: Cardinality

  // Authored annotations (what the user wrote, verbatim)
  type_annotation: AuthoredNode?
  transport: AuthoredNode?
  uses: List<AuthoredNode>
  properties: List<AuthoredNode>

  // Expression kind (syntactic form, NOT semantic classification)
  expr_kind: ExprKind
  match_pattern: MatchPattern?
}

// ExprKind is PURELY STRUCTURAL. No binding_kind, no call_semantics,
// no method_semantics, no field_summary. Those are semantic facts.
type ExprKind =
    Literal { value: LiteralValue }
  | Var
  | FieldAccess
  | Call
  | MethodCall
  | Match | If | Let | Block
  | RecordLit | ListLit
  | BinOp { op: BinOp }
  | UnaryOp { op: UnaryOpKind }
  | Lambda | StringInterp | ForEach
  | Index | Slice | Cast | Return

type AuthoredGraph {
  modules: List<AuthoredModule>
  source_indices: Map<String, NewlineIndex>
  intern_table: InternTable
}

type AuthoredModule {
  root: AuthoredNode
  resolved_imports: List<ResolvedImport>
  dep_order: Int
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3b. Semantic graph
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The real compiler boundary. Where "illegal states unrepresentable"
// becomes true.
//
// OWNS:
//   - stable semantic ids for expressions, bindings, items, and types
//   - resolved reference kind (value / function / type / variant / field / method)
//   - resolved type shape (structure, not a name that may later resolve)
//   - field-access classification (stored / enum accessor / optional unwrap / tuple)
//   - method/intrinsic semantics (closed enum, not string dispatch)
//   - call semantics (direct / builtin / service)
//   - binding identity across patterns and branches
//   - expected type context where required for downstream correctness
//   - cardinality and type obligations
//   - descent and size evidence (enough for complexity without heuristics)
//   - consumer / fan-out facts (enough for ownership without recovery)
//   - transport / service obligations
//   - provenance links back to authored nodes/spans
//
// DOES NOT OWN:
//   - authored names as semantic authority
//   - target-language rendering choices
//   - Rust/Go/Python policy
//
// A consumer must not:
//   - guess whether a reference names a value, function, type, or variant
//   - infer optional-unwrapping from a field name
//   - branch on "Unit" or "Dynamic" because expected-type propagation
//     never arrived
//   - compensate for missing fold accumulator types or empty literal types

type SemanticNode {
  id: NodeId
  span: SourceSpan                   // carried for diagnostics

  // Resolved structure
  children: List<SemanticNode>
  connective: Connective
  params: List<SemanticNode>
  body: SemanticNode?
  return_cardinality: Cardinality

  // Resolved type (not Optional — errors are diagnostics, not IR holes)
  resolved_type: ResolvedType

  // Expression semantics (fully classified — no None slots)
  expr: ExprSemantics

  // Dimension values (provenance, ownership, effects, cost)
  dimensions: DimensionValues
}

// ResolvedType is a resolved structure, not a name.
// If inference failed, the type is ErrorType and a diagnostic was emitted.
// Downstream stages never see "unresolved."
type ResolvedType =
    Concrete { shape: TypeShape }
  | ErrorType { message: String }
  | TypeVar { id: Int }

type TypeShape {
  kind: TypeKind
  children: List<TypeShape>
  fields: List<FieldShape>
  variants: List<VariantShape>
}

type TypeKind =
    Primitive { name: PrimitiveType }
  | Record { name: String }
  | Sum { name: String }
  | Container { kind: ContainerKind }
  | Function { arity: Int }
  | Optional
  | Unit

type FieldShape {
  name: String
  type_shape: TypeShape
  cardinality: Cardinality
  default: SemanticNode?
}

type VariantShape {
  name: String
  fields: List<FieldShape>
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3b-i. Mandatory semantic facts
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This list is not hypothetical. It comes from currently live pain points.
// A semantic expression node must carry, directly or through typed edges:

// Reference classification — no guessing value vs function vs type.
type BindingRef {
  id: BindingId
  kind: BindingKind
  source_name: String               // provenance only, NOT semantic authority
}

type BindingKind =
    LocalValue
  | FunctionValue
  | VariantConstructor
  | MatchBound                       // including VariantPattern destructuring
  | ForEachElement

// Field access classification — no downstream repair.
type FieldRef {
  name: String
  access: FieldAccessKind
  value_shape: FieldValueShape
  declaring_type: TypeRef
}

type FieldAccessKind =
    StoredField
  | EnumAccessor
  | OptionalUnwrap
  | TupleFirst
  | TupleSecond

// Method semantics — closed enum, not string dispatch.
type MethodRef =
    PlainMethod { name: String, resolved_def: TypeRef }
  | AlgebraMethod { name: String, algebra_field: AlgebraFieldKind, method_def: TypeRef, fold_accumulator: TypeShape? }
  | ServiceMethod { name: String, operation: ServiceOperation }

// Call semantics — no fabrication from return types.
type CalleeRef =
    DirectCall { target: BindingRef }
  | BuiltinCall { builtin: BuiltinKind }

// Descent evidence — computed at the boundary, not reconstructed by CX.
type DescentEvidence =
    StructuralDescent { relation: SubValueRelation }
  | NoRecursion
  | Unresolved                       // boundary couldn't determine → diagnostic

// ExprSemantics — fully classified. No None slots. No Optional wrappers
// that "inference will fill in later." If a fact can't be determined,
// it's an ErrorType with a diagnostic.
type ExprSemantics =
    Literal { value: LiteralValue }
  | Var { binding: BindingRef }
  | FieldAccess { receiver: SemanticNode, field: FieldRef }
  | Call { callee: CalleeRef, args: List<SemanticNode>, descent: DescentEvidence }
  | MethodCall { receiver: SemanticNode, method: MethodRef, args: List<SemanticNode> }
  | Match { scrutinee: SemanticNode, arms: List<MatchArm> }
  | If { condition: SemanticNode, then_branch: SemanticNode, else_branch: SemanticNode? }
  | Let { binding: BindingDef, value: SemanticNode, body: SemanticNode }
  | Block { statements: List<SemanticNode> }
  | RecordLit { type_ref: TypeRef, fields: List<FieldInit> }
  | ListLit { elements: List<SemanticNode> }
  | BinOp { op: BinOp, left: SemanticNode, right: SemanticNode, algebra: AlgebraRef? }
  | UnaryOp { op: UnaryOpKind, operand: SemanticNode }
  | Lambda { params: List<LambdaParam>, body: SemanticNode }
  | StringInterp { parts: List<StringPart> }
  | ForEach { collection: SemanticNode, variable: BindingDef, body: SemanticNode }
  | Index { collection: SemanticNode, index: SemanticNode }
  | Slice { collection: SemanticNode, start: SemanticNode?, end: SemanticNode? }
  | Cast { value: SemanticNode, target_type: TypeShape }
  | Return { value: SemanticNode? }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3b-ii. Dimension values
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Computed at binding sites during authored_to_semantic.
// Consumed downstream as settled facts. Never reconstructed.
//
// Fixed struct for v3. Generic mechanism (user-defined dimensions)
// is v3.1, after concrete instances validate the pattern.

type DimensionValues {
  provenance: SubValueRelation       // how this value relates to its source
  ownership: OwnershipFact           // consumption pattern
  effect: EffectShape                // side effect classification
  cost: CostBound                    // computation cost bound
}

type OwnershipFact {
  fan_out: Int
  last_use: BindingId?
  consumption: ConsumptionKind
}

type ConsumptionKind =
    Consumed | Read | Projected | Threaded

type CostBound =
    Constant { value: Int }
  | Linear { dimension: String }
  | Symbolic { expr: CostExpr }
  | Unknown

type EffectShape =
    Pure
  | ServiceCall { transport: TransportKind }
  | Mutation { target: BindingRef }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3b-iii. Bidirectional inference
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// v2's inference is top-down only. Expected types don't propagate into
// sub-expressions. This causes emit to compensate for empty_map() types,
// fold accumulator types, and lambda parameter types.
//
// v3's authored_to_semantic boundary uses bidirectional inference:
// expected types flow DOWN, inferred types flow UP. By the time we
// leave this boundary, every expression has complete type context.

type InferContext {
  expected_type: TypeShape?          // from annotation or downstream usage
  inferred_type: TypeShape           // from the expression itself
  scope: ScopeTable                  // keyed by BindingId, not name string
}

type ScopeTable {
  bindings: Map<BindingId, BindingEntry>
  types: Map<TypeId, TypeShape>
}

type BindingEntry {
  id: BindingId
  kind: BindingKind
  resolved_type: TypeShape
  dimensions: DimensionValues
  source_name: String                // provenance only
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3c. Analysis views
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Ownership and complexity are read-only projections over the semantic
// graph. They do not invent new semantic guesses, create parallel fact
// tables, or set target rendering policy.
//
// OWNERSHIP consumes semantic binding/use facts and emits proofs or
// diagnostics. It does not reclassify references from names or renderer
// syntax. It must cover destructuring and variant-pattern bindings, not
// just simple locals. The fallback model "try unwrap else clone" is the
// wrong shape — the compiler proves the move or fails structurally.
//
// COMPLEXITY consumes semantic descent, size, and expected-type facts.
// It does not compensate for missing inference. Fold refinement and
// empty-literal gaps are upstream incompleteness that v3 forbids from
// leaking past the semantic boundary.

fn complexity_view(graph: SemanticGraph) -> ComplexityReport
fn ownership_view(graph: SemanticGraph, target: LanguageSpec) -> OwnershipPolicy

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3d. Render plan
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The last boundary before target text.
//
// OWNS:
//   - target-agnostic rendering intents
//   - type rendering descriptors (fully resolved target-language types)
//   - import/module requirements
//   - sharing/copy strategy (derived from LanguageSpec + ownership facts)
//   - target-specific lowering choices from LanguageSpec
//
// DOES NOT OWN:
//   - unresolved meaning
//   - target-name guesses
//   - policy hidden in core semantics
//
// Emission is translation, not decision-making. The emitter is a pure
// function from (RenderPlan) to text. Known decisions that must move
// into data or precomputed descriptors: sharing, clone semantics,
// option/absence, async syntax, imports, container iteration, record
// wrapping.

fn semantic_to_render(graph: SemanticGraph, spec: LanguageSpec) -> RenderPlan

type RenderPlan {
  modules: List<RenderModule>
  imports: List<ImportDecl>
}

type RenderModule {
  items: List<RenderItem>
}

type RenderItem =
    RenderType { shape: TypeRender }
  | RenderFunction { sig: FunctionRender, body: ExprRender }
  | RenderService { sig: FunctionRender, operations: List<OperationRender> }
  | RenderData { name: String, value: ExprRender }

type TypeRender {
  target_text: String                // fully resolved, e.g. "Rc<Vec<Rc<Node>>>"
  value_context: ValueContext
  sharing: SharingStrategy
}

type ValueContext =
    ConstantData | RuntimeValue | SpecificationWitness | CallableValue

type SharingStrategy =
    Move | Clone | Borrow | Copy | GarbageCollected

type ExprRender =
    LiteralRender { text: String }
  | VarRender { name: String, sharing: SharingStrategy }
  | FieldAccessRender { receiver: ExprRender, field: String, access: FieldAccessKind }
  | CallRender { callee: String, args: List<ExprRender> }
  | MethodCallRender { receiver: ExprRender, method: String, args: List<ExprRender> }
  | MatchRender { scrutinee: ExprRender, arms: List<ArmRender> }
  | IfRender { condition: ExprRender, then_expr: ExprRender, else_expr: ExprRender? }
  | LetRender { name: String, type_text: String, value: ExprRender, body: ExprRender }
  | BlockRender { statements: List<ExprRender> }
  | RecordLitRender { type_name: String, fields: List<FieldInitRender> }
  | ListLitRender { elements: List<ExprRender> }
  | BinOpRender { left: ExprRender, op: String, right: ExprRender }
  | UnaryOpRender { op: String, operand: ExprRender }
  | LambdaRender { params: List<ParamRender>, body: ExprRender }
  | StringInterpRender { parts: List<StringPartRender> }
  | ForEachRender { var_name: String, collection: ExprRender, body: ExprRender }
  | IndexRender { collection: ExprRender, index: ExprRender }
  | SliceRender { collection: ExprRender, start: ExprRender?, end: ExprRender? }
  | CastRender { value: ExprRender, target_text: String }
  | ReturnRender { value: ExprRender? }
  | TcoLoopRender { params: List<ParamRender>, body: ExprRender }

// =========================================================================
// 4. HARD RULES
// =========================================================================
//
// These are the repo invariants restated as design constraints.
//
// MUST:
//   - keep authored names only in authored/provenance space
//   - make every boundary fact exact, typed, and consumed downstream
//   - keep core target-agnostic
//   - treat render as a consumer of semantic truth plus LanguageSpec
//   - fail closed when a required semantic fact is absent
//
// MUST NOT:
//   - use one universal Node schema across all stages
//   - introduce a new fact table without a same-change consumer
//   - use authored names for structural dispatch after the authored layer
//   - put Rust/Go/Python policy in shared semantic types
//   - let emit recover from missing semantics by heuristics or fabrication

// =========================================================================
// 5. EXPRESSION-WALK RULE
// =========================================================================
//
// v2 caps full ExprData walks at five (ratchet, containment). v3
// promotes a first-class fold/visitor to compiler architecture.
//
// No stage writes its own expression walker. All stages use fold_expr.
// When ExprSemantics grows by one variant, every consumer gets a
// compile error (non-exhaustive match) rather than silently skipping
// the new case.
//
// Adding a new full expression walker is PROHIBITED without deleting
// or merging an existing one.

fn fold_expr<A>(
  node: SemanticNode,
  init: A,
  on_literal: fn(A, LiteralValue) -> A,
  on_var: fn(A, BindingRef) -> A,
  on_field_access: fn(A, SemanticNode, FieldRef) -> A,
  on_call: fn(A, CalleeRef, List<SemanticNode>, DescentEvidence) -> A,
  on_method_call: fn(A, SemanticNode, MethodRef, List<SemanticNode>) -> A,
  on_match: fn(A, SemanticNode, List<MatchArm>) -> A,
  on_if: fn(A, SemanticNode, SemanticNode, SemanticNode?) -> A,
  on_let: fn(A, BindingDef, SemanticNode, SemanticNode) -> A,
  on_block: fn(A, List<SemanticNode>) -> A,
  on_record_lit: fn(A, TypeRef, List<FieldInit>) -> A,
  on_list_lit: fn(A, List<SemanticNode>) -> A,
  on_bin_op: fn(A, BinOp, SemanticNode, SemanticNode) -> A,
  on_unary_op: fn(A, UnaryOpKind, SemanticNode) -> A,
  on_lambda: fn(A, List<LambdaParam>, SemanticNode) -> A,
  on_string_interp: fn(A, List<StringPart>) -> A,
  on_for_each: fn(A, SemanticNode, BindingDef, SemanticNode) -> A,
  on_index: fn(A, SemanticNode, SemanticNode) -> A,
  on_slice: fn(A, SemanticNode, SemanticNode?, SemanticNode?) -> A,
  on_cast: fn(A, SemanticNode, TypeShape) -> A,
  on_return: fn(A, SemanticNode?) -> A
) -> A

// =========================================================================
// 6. BOOTSTRAP CLAUSE
// =========================================================================
//
// Bootstrap is architecture, not tooling. v3 is not "done" unless the
// bootstrap path is authoritative and reproducible.
//
// Contracts:
//   - generated stage0 is derived, never hand-edited
//   - the owned bootstrap command runs sanity gates, emitted-Rust gate,
//     regeneration, and fixed-point checks
//   - CI enforces regenerate → diff → empty
//   - broader structural work resumes only after bootstrap is green
//
// Bootstrap reports per-stage structural diffs when divergence occurs.
// "Fixed point failed" without identifying the divergent stage is not
// acceptable.

type BootstrapResult {
  converged: Bool
  stage_diffs: List<StageDiff>
}

type StageDiff {
  stage_name: String
  old_output_hash: String
  new_output_hash: String
  changed_items: List<String>
}

// =========================================================================
// 7. ACCEPTANCE TESTS (ratchets, not prose)
// =========================================================================
//
// T1: Scrambled-name test
//     Rename authored identifiers in AuthoredGraph → SemanticGraph.
//     Semantic and render decisions must not change.
//
// T2: No speculative boundaries
//     Any new semantic table or pass must have a same-change downstream
//     consumer. Fact tables without consumers are violations.
//
// T3: No target leakage in core
//     Core types must satisfy: "would this make sense for VHDL?"
//     Rust/Go/Python-specific policy belongs in LanguageSpec data.
//
// T4: No new full expr walk
//     Without deleting or merging an existing one. Ratchet: fold_expr
//     is the sole expression traversal API.
//
// T5: Emit compile errors are upstream bugs
//     Emitted code should fail only when earlier stages already surfaced
//     the owning diagnostic. If emit hits a case it can't handle and no
//     prior diagnostic exists, that's a semantic boundary bug.
//
// T6: Bootstrap fixed point
//     Owned bootstrap entrypoint + CI regen diff gate.
//     Regenerate → diff → empty, or the build fails.

// =========================================================================
// 8. PIPELINE
// =========================================================================
//
//   Source text
//     │
//     ▼ tokenize
//   Token stream
//     │
//     ▼ parse + resolve
//   AuthoredGraph           (what the user wrote)
//     │
//     ▼ authored_to_semantic (inference + bidirectional types + dimensions)
//   SemanticGraph            (what the compiler proved)
//     │
//     ├──▶ complexity_view   (read descent + cost, compose, check)
//     ├──▶ ownership_view    (read fan_out + last_use, derive policy)
//     │
//     ▼ semantic_to_render   (+ LanguageSpec → target decisions)
//   RenderPlan               (what the emitter translates)
//     │
//     ▼ render               (string concatenation — no decisions)
//   TextFile

fn compile(sources: List<SourceFile>, target: LanguageSpec) -> PipelineResult {
  let tokens = sources |> map(s => tokenize(source: s.content, file: s.path))
  let authored = parse_and_resolve(tokens: tokens)
  let semantic = authored_to_semantic(authored: authored)
  let complexity = complexity_view(graph: semantic)
  let ownership = ownership_view(graph: semantic, target: target)
  let plan = semantic_to_render(graph: semantic, spec: target)
  let files = render(plan: plan)

  PipelineResult {
    files: files,
    diagnostics: semantic.diagnostics ++ complexity.diagnostics ++ ownership.diagnostics,
    complexity: complexity,
    ownership: ownership
  }
}

// =========================================================================
// 9. IMPLEMENTATION ORDER
// =========================================================================
//
// Fix the deepest unsound ancestor first, not the compensating leaf.
//
// 1. Specify AuthoredGraph and SemanticGraph types.
// 2. Land the missing semantic facts for currently live boundary leaks.
// 3. Move ownership and complexity to pure semantic consumers.
// 4. Introduce RenderPlan + LanguageSpec-driven rendering.
// 5. Lock the bootstrap fixed-point loop and CI gate.

// =========================================================================
// 10. DEFERRED TO V3.1+
// =========================================================================
//
// - Generic dimension mechanism (user-defined dimensions via type-indexed
//   map). v3 uses fixed DimensionValues. Generalize after concrete
//   instances validate the pattern.
//
// - Interpreter. v2's interpreter is clean. It reads transport specs
//   like the emitter. Operates on SemanticGraph with a new entry point.
//
// - Single emitter consolidation. RenderPlan makes this mechanical:
//   one render function reads the plan. Per-language differences are in
//   LanguageSpec data, not emitter code.
//
// - Omni-emission. Falls out when single emitter works. Pipeline runs
//   once through SemanticGraph; semantic_to_render runs per target.
//   Analysis views run once. Only rendering is per-target.
